{
  "dom_id": "step-q163683",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3})-({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =4+\\frac{8}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
